-- DBTITLE 1,Workspace/Home
-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### 3.1 ① Workspace/Home (🏠)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Each user has their own workspace which serves as a personal document and code storage area. It contains a user folder which is only accessible to that user by default, along with any Git repositories that you have cloned or created within Databricks.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Your workspace can be accessed in the Databricks sidebar.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Home page](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Everything in your workspace is only accessible to you unless you share it with other users. When you do share a script or file you can specify whether the person you’re sharing it with is able to view/edit/run the file you’re sharing.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### How workspaces are organised
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC A **workspace** is your Databricks environment, it's where you write code, manage data, and run jobs.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC **What's in a workspace?**
-- MAGIC - **User folders**: Each user has a personal folder (`/Users/your.email@education.gov.uk/`)
-- MAGIC - **Shared folders**: Team or project folders that multiple users can access
-- MAGIC - **Repos/Git folders**: Cloned repositories from Azure DevOps or GitHub
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC **Workspace organisation at DfE**
-- MAGIC - Workspaces are typically separated by **environment** (dev, test, prod) or **team** (see ② Workspace dropdown below)
-- MAGIC - Your personal folder is your sandbox, experiment freely here
-- MAGIC - Production/shared work should live in Git-controlled repos
-- MAGIC - Keep your workspace tidy: use folders, meaningful names, and archive old work
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Best practices
-- MAGIC - **Don't store data in your workspace** - use Unity Catalog tables or Volumes
-- MAGIC - **Use Git folders** for anything that needs version control or collaboration
-- MAGIC - **Name notebooks clearly** and include numbers for ordering (e.g., `01_data_load`, `02_transform`)
-- MAGIC - **Share via permissions**, not by copying files into other people's folders
-- MAGIC - **Share Notebooks only to others with access or clear all cell outputs**, if you share a notebook using the Databricks interface, cell outputs remain intact from the last time you ran the code. This risks sharing data that should not be shared with others. Before sharing code with someone outside your team, you must use the Clear all cell outputs option in the Databricks “Run” menu to avoid inadvertently sharing data that only you and your team should have access to.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ---
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ### ✅ Task: Set up your workspace
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Follow these steps to organise your personal workspace:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. In the left sidebar, right-click **Workspace** and open in a new tab - can you see your recent items and your user folder?
-- MAGIC 2. Click **Workspace** and navigate to your user folder (`/Users/your.email@education.gov.uk/`)
-- MAGIC 3. Create a new folder by clicking > **Create** > **Folder** and name it something like `workshop_practice`
-- MAGIC 4. Inside your new folder, create a new **Notebook** (Create > Notebook)
-- MAGIC 5. Give it a descriptive name (e.g., `01_my_first_notebook`)
-- MAGIC 6. Try changing the notebook's default language using the dropdown at the top
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC **Questions to consider:**
-- MAGIC - Can you see other people's folders? Why or why not?
-- MAGIC - Where would you store code that your team needs to collaborate on? (Hint: not here!)
-- MAGIC - What's the difference between storing something in your workspace vs. in a Git folder?

