-- DBTITLE 1,Catalog
-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### 3.3 ③ Catalog (Unity Catalog structure)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The majority of data and files on Databricks should be stored in the ‘unity catalog’. This is similar in concept to a traditional database server, however the unity catalog also contains file storage in the form of volumes.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The unity catalog can be accessed through the ‘Catalog’ option in the Databricks sidebar.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Catalog Explorer](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC There is one ‘unity catalog’ for the whole of the Department for Education, this is what enables the Department to keep track of all of its data in a single place. The ‘unity catalog’ can contain any number of catalogs (equivalent to databases). Catalogs are very similar in concept to a SQL database in that they contain schemas, tables of data and views of data.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Unity Catalog](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC If you can't see the catalog you are looking for, you may need to change to the right environment (see ② Workspace dropdown above).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Unity Catalog organises all data in a **three-level namespace**:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ```
-- MAGIC Catalog > Schema (Database) > Tables/Views/Volumes
-- MAGIC ```
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC For example below shows the 'catalog_40_copper_analyst_training' contains 9 schemas and the schema 'bronze' contains 10 tables:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Schemas](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC You'll be able to see in the screenshot above that there are different layers being identified: Copper, Bronze, Silver, and Gold — each representing a level of quality and refinement. This is called The Medallion architecture and will be covered in more detail later in the course.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Structure
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Like a SQL database a catalog has schemas, tables, and views which store data in a structured (usually tabular) format.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - **Catalog** - Top-level container (e.g. `catalog_name`). Often represents an environment or department.
-- MAGIC - **Schema** - Groups related tables together (e.g. `schema_name`) It is a sub-division of a catalog which allows for logical separation of data stored in the catalog. 
-- MAGIC - **Tables** - The actual data, equivalent to SQL tables and have the ability to turn on version control which audits each change to the data. referenced as `catalog.schema.table`
-- MAGIC - **Views** - Saved SQL queries that act like tables but don't store data themselves; the query runs each time the view is referenced, allowing alternative formats without duplicating data
-- MAGIC - **Volumes** - File storage similar to a shared drive, used for storing any type of file (CSVs, JSON, images, etc.) that can be accessed and manipulated through code
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Tables and views sit within a schema and these are where you would store your core datasets and pick up data to analyse from.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Volumes are stored under a schema within a catalog. Files in here can be accessed and manipulated through code. You can also upload files to a volume through the user interface.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Permissions
-- MAGIC - Access is controlled at catalog, schema, or table level
-- MAGIC - You can only see/query data you've been granted access to
-- MAGIC - Use the **Catalog Explorer** (left sidebar > Catalog) to browse what's available to you
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC More information on Unity Catalog, Schemas and Tables can be found in the [Analysts' Guide](https://dfe-analytical-services.github.io/analysts-guide/ADA/databricks_fundamentals.html#unity-catalog)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ---
-- MAGIC
