-- DBTITLE 1,SQL Editor
-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### 3.4 ④ SQL Editor
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC You can write and run new SQL code in the Databricks SQL Editor, which is very similar to SSMS. These queries are automatically saved as workspace objects while you're working on them. Databricks also provides version history and reopens your last query when you return. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC We recommend using the SQL Editor for short, ad-hoc SQL queries, such as those required to answer PQs. For longer or more complex SQL analysis we recommend using standalone scripts run from an RStudio project, or Databricks notebooks.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![SQL Editor](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Writing and running SQL queries in SQL Editor has only the SQL Warehouse compute option available (see ⑥ Compute below). Also the language used in Databricks is Spark SQL syntax which is a different dialect to that used in SSMS, so your current SSMS code may need tweaking. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC There is detailed guidance on using the Databricks SQL editor, on the [ADA guidance site](https://analytical-data-access.education.gov.uk/get-support/using-sql-in-databricks-sql-editor).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ---
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ### ✅ Task: Open the SQL Editor
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. Right-click **SQL Editor** in the left sidebarand open in a new tab
-- MAGIC 2. What compute does it connect to? (Check the dropdown at the top)

