-- DBTITLE 1,Compute
-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### 3.5 ⑥ Compute (SQL warehouses, personal and shared clusters)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC You can manage your clusters and SQL warehouses in Compute, accessed through the ‘Compute’ option in the Databricks sidebar.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Compute](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In order to access data and run code you need to set up a compute resource. A compute resource provides processing power and memory to pick up and manipulate the data and files stored in the ‘unity catalog’.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In Databricks, **"compute"** is the processing power that runs your code, and a **"cluster"** is the collection of computing resources that actually runs your code, queries, and data processing workloads.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### How is this different from SSMS?
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC If someone's computer crashes mid-query locally (in RStudio/SSMS) the query stops immediately, then they lose any temporary results and have to start again from scratch.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC But on Databricks someone's computer crashing has no effect on a running query. The processing is happening on the cluster (in Azure cloud), not on the machine. So closing it, losing connection, or crashing doesn't interrupt execution. You can reopen Databricks on another device and the query will either still be running or have completed, with results.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Also, in SSMS, you connect to a SQL Server that someone else manages, a fixed server shared across your organisation. You don't choose how powerful it is, and if lots of people are running heavy queries at the same time, everyone slows down. You also have no visibility of what resources are being used.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In Databricks, compute is **flexible and user-controlled**:
-- MAGIC - You choose the size and type of compute that fits your task
-- MAGIC - Compute can scale up (more power) or out (more machines working in parallel) depending on demand
-- MAGIC - Multiple users can have separate compute resources, so you're not competing for the same fixed server
-- MAGIC - Heavy workloads can be spread across a **cluster of machines** working together, something a single SQL Server can't do
-- MAGIC - You only pay for what you use, and it shuts down automatically when idle
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC The trade-off is that you have to be a bit more aware of what compute you're using and why, which is what this section covers.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Compute options
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC | Compute type | Purpose / Use cases | Key features / Notes | Configuration | Code language support | Availability |
-- MAGIC | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
-- MAGIC | Serverless | Quick start to query in SQL or Python | Minimal warm-up time. No infrastructure to manage. No query can run longer than 48 hours | Handled by Databricks | SQL, Python | All workspace users |
-- MAGIC | SQL Warehouse | Running SQL queries and powering dashboards. Best for analysts writing SQL, BI tools, scheduled queries. Only compute option for SQL Editor | Auto-scales, auto-suspends, optimised for SQL workloads. Shared resource so more efficient. Relatively short boot up time. Recommended compute for any SQL activities | Created by ADA team for each catalog | SQL | Only other users of same warehouse (typically your team) |
-- MAGIC | Personal Cluster | Interactive development for a single user. Exploratory work in Python/R/Scala, ML experimentation. Only option for R coding. Use when you need specific versions or libraries | Single-user access, full library control. Install packages, choose runtime version. Shuts down after being unused for an hour. Least cost effective so only use if needed. Takes a few minutes to boot up. Compute power limited to standard or large but can be raised if necessary | Set up by you with choice of standard (roughly same power as DfE laptop) or large (roughly double standard) | SQL, Python, R, Scala | Only you |
-- MAGIC | Shared Cluster | Shared resource to be more cost effective. Where using SQL, Python or Scala please use this or Serverless | Multiple users share resources, managed by admins. Less control over packages/config vs. personal clusters. Takes a few minutes to boot up | Created by ADA team for specific projects | SQL, Python, Scala | Other users on same project |
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Which should I use?
-- MAGIC | Task | Recommended compute |
-- MAGIC | --- | --- |
-- MAGIC | Quick ad-hoc SQL or Python queries | Serverless |
-- MAGIC | SQL queries, dashboards, and SQL Editor | SQL Warehouse* |
-- MAGIC | Python or Scala development | Shared Cluster or Serverless |
-- MAGIC | R coding or need specific libraries/versions | Personal Cluster or Shared Cluster |
-- MAGIC | Team project work (Python/Scala) | Shared Cluster |
-- MAGIC | Scheduled production jobs | Job Cluster (auto-created) |
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC *You should use SQL warehouse unless:
-- MAGIC - You need to read in data from Databricks volumes
-- MAGIC - There are likely to be multiple people running intensive processes in your team (who are on the same SQL warehouse) at the same time, to the point it would hit the processing limit
-- MAGIC - You/your team don't have access to a SQL warehouse 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC > **Cost note:** Clusters cost money while running. Always let them auto-terminate when not in use. SQL warehouses auto-suspend after a few minutes of inactivity.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Note that shared clusters and SQL warehouses have to be requested through the [ADA team](adaptteam@education.gov.uk).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In most cases a personal cluster will be the most versatile and easily accessible option. However your team may want to request a shared SQL warehouse if you have a lot of processing heavy SQL queries and do not need to use any other language in the Databricks platform itself.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC All compute options can be used both within the Databricks platform and be connected to through other applications. Instructions on how to connect R / RStudio to a SQL Warehouse, or a personal cluster can be found on the following pages:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC - [Setup Databricks SQL Warehouse with RStudio](https://dfe-analytical-services.github.io/analysts-guide/ADA/databricks_rstudio_sql_warehouse.html)
-- MAGIC - [Setup Databricks Personal Compute cluster with RStudio](https://dfe-analytical-services.github.io/analysts-guide/ADA/databricks_rstudio_personal_cluster.html)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Once you have a compute resource you can begin using Databricks. You can do this either through connecting to Databricks through RStudio / Positron / your preferred IDE, or you can begin coding in the Databricks platforms using scripts, or Notebooks.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ---
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ### ✅ Task: Set up a Personal Cluster
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC First, click **Compute** in the left sidebar - can you see any clusters or SQL warehouses already available to you?
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Then follow these steps to create your own personal compute cluster:
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC 1. In the left sidebar, right-click **Compute** and **Open link in new tab**
-- MAGIC 2. Click the **Create with DfE Personal Compute** button (top right)
-- MAGIC 3. Under **Policy**, select **DfE Personal Compute**
-- MAGIC 4. Give your cluster a name (e.g., `SURNAME, Firstname's DfE Personal Compute Cluster`)
-- MAGIC 5. Under **Preferred node type**, choose your cluster size:
-- MAGIC    - **Standard**, roughly the same power as a DfE laptop (suitable for most tasks)
-- MAGIC    - **Large**, roughly double the power of standard (for heavier workloads)  
-- MAGIC 6. In Access mode, click 'Manual' and 'Dedicated (formerly: Single User)' as you will then be able to code in all languages
-- MAGIC 7. Leave other settings as default unless you have a specific need
-- MAGIC 8. Click **Create**
-- MAGIC 9. Wait a few minutes for the cluster to start, you'll see the status change from "Pending" to "Running"
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Databricks charges based on the usage of compute, so if you finish work on Databricks it is good practice to terminate it when you're done to save money for the Department. As a precaution against running up unnecessary charges your personal cluster is set to terminate after 1 hour of inactivity.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC #### Things to note
-- MAGIC - Your cluster will **automatically shut down** after 60 minutes of inactivity
-- MAGIC - To restart it later, go to **Compute** and click the **Start** button next to your cluster
-- MAGIC - You can **terminate** your cluster manually when you're finished to save costs
-- MAGIC - If you need to install specific libraries, you can do so via the **Libraries** tab on your cluster's page

-- COMMAND ----------

-- DBTITLE 1,Jobs & Pipelines
-- MAGIC %md
-- MAGIC ### 3.6 ⑤ Jobs & Pipelines
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In Databricks you can schedule and monitor automated pipelines.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC If you’d like to make use of automated workflows to run scheduled code, it can make use of code from notebooks, R or SQL scripts stored in either a Databricks workspace or a Git repository. We recommend using a Git repository for most projects.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC You can read more about workflows on our [Databricks workflows page](https://dfe-analytical-services.github.io/analysts-guide/ADA/databricks_workflows.html).
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC ![Workflows](data:image/png;base64,)
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC In addition to setting up workflows using the point and click interface in Databricks, you can also script them yourself. 
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC To learn more about this, see our [script workflows in Databricks](https://dfe-analytical-services.github.io/analysts-guide/ADA/databricks_workflow_script_databricks.html) user guide.
-- MAGIC
-- MAGIC Jobs & Pipelines are covered in more detail in the '04 Using Jobs & Pipelines' section of this workshop.

